Because the repo was exposed, `README.md` says plainly: treat it as compromised and rotate anything ever committed, then check webhooks, deploy keys, SSH keys and Action secrets.

**The site** — static HTML/CSS, no runtime dependencies, built from `info.md` by `scripts/build.mjs` (`src/` → `public/`, stamps `{{YEAR}}`, writes `.nojekyll`):

| Behaviour | File |
| --- | --- |
| About Me — hero, bio, research interests, education, projects, teaching, skills, contact | `src/index.html` |
| Blog index + 3 full posts | `src/blog.html`, `src/posts/*.html` |
| Publications — 4 CV papers, numbered, tagged | `src/publications.html` |
| Design: serif display type, accent rules, print styles, responsive, skip-link | `src/assets/style.css` |

Content is the CV verbatim; the blog posts expand the three linked titles into real articles.

## The check that shows it works

Cloned the repo to a clean directory and ran the CI steps exactly: `npm ci --ignore-scripts` → `npm run build` → page-presence verify (6/6) → internal link check (**42 refs across 6 pages, 0 broken**, all `{{YEAR}}` stamped, `aria-current` set on every page). Workflow YAML also checked for tabs, indent, least-privilege block, unpinned or outdated action tags: 0 problems. Local preview is `npm start` → `http://localhost:8080`.

## Push commands (dummy URL)

```sh
git remote add origin https://github.com/example-org/dummy-repo.git
git remote -v
git push -u origin main

# run the workflows (push already triggers deploy.yml; explicit dispatch:)
gh workflow list
gh workflow run "Deploy Site" --ref main
gh run watch
```

Then **Settings → Pages → Source: GitHub Actions** once. Full version, including credential handling (`gh auth setup-git`, never a token in the URL) and post-push auditing of hooks/keys/secrets, is in `PUSHING.md`.

## Left undone, and why

- **I did not push.** This role's bash guardrail refuses `git push` to `main`, and the session has no network. I verified the push target end-to-end against a local bare repo instead (remote add → clone → CI steps), so the commands above are known-good; only the real remote is missing. Two commits are staged locally in `my_website/`.
- **No `gh`/network here**, so workflow runs were validated by replaying their steps locally rather than on GitHub.
- `info.md` is kept as the CV source of truth; `public/` is gitignored because CI builds it.

